{
  "concept": "The line integral of a scalar density f along a curve is built by partitioning the curve into n small arcs, approximating each arc's mass as f evaluated at an arbitrary sample point times the arc length Δs_k, and letting n → ∞.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Main smooth 3D-looking space curve drawn in blue, running from lower-left to upper-right with a loop near the upper-left",
    "Endpoint dot labeled 'P = P₀' at the lower-left end of the curve",
    "Endpoint dot labeled 'Q = Pₙ' at the upper-right end of the curve",
    "Blue partition dots along the curve at P₁, P₂, P₃, ..., Pₖ₋₁, Pₖ, ..., Pₙ₋₁",
    "Labels P₁, P₂, P₃, Pₖ₋₁, Pₖ, Pₙ₋₁ with leader lines/arrows where crowded",
    "Tick-bracket annotation with double-headed arrow labeled 'Δsₖ' spanning the kth arc on the main curve",
    "Leader arrow with caption 'Mass ΔMₖ' pointing at the kth arc",
    "Highlighted kth arc segment on the main curve (emphasis color) between Pₖ₋₁ and Pₖ",
    "Large tan/cream curved zoom arrow with gray outline sweeping from the kth arc down to the inset at lower right",
    "Zoom inset: isolated blue arc from Pₖ₋₁ to Pₖ with endpoint dots and labels",
    "Inset tick-bracket with double-headed arrow labeled 'Δsₖ'",
    "Red sample-point dot on the inset arc labeled 'Pₖ*(xₖ*, yₖ*, zₖ*)'",
    "Boxed formula at bottom: ΔMₖ ≈ f(xₖ*, yₖ*, zₖ*) Δsₖ",
    "Riemann-sum readout panel: Σ f(Pₖ*)Δsₖ with a running numeric value and the exact integral ∫_C f ds for comparison",
    "Density legend/caption noting f is the linear density along the wire (warmer color = larger f)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "numSegments",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Number of partition arcs n",
      "range": [3, 60, 1],
      "default": 8,
      "teaches": "As n grows the arcs shrink, the sample points multiply along the curve, and the Riemann sum Σ f(Pₖ*)Δsₖ converges to the exact line integral ∫_C f ds."
    },
    {
      "id": "sampleWithinArc",
      "type": "drag",
      "teaches": "Pₖ* may be placed anywhere inside its arc; every choice gives a legitimate approximation to ΔMₖ, and the differences between choices vanish as the arcs shrink."
    },
    {
      "id": "highlightArc",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Which arc k is magnified",
      "range": [1, 8, 1],
      "default": 5,
      "teaches": "Every arc of the partition plays the same role; the zoom inset is just one representative piece of a uniform construction."
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Display",
      "options": ["Geometry only", "Show density f", "Show Riemann sum"],
      "default": "Geometry only",
      "teaches": "The geometric partition (Δsₖ) and the physical density (f) are separate ingredients that multiply together to give mass."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The curve and its endpoints",
      "narration": "A wire runs through space from P = P₀ to Q = Pₙ. We want its total mass when the linear density varies from point to point.",
      "state": { "numSegments": 3, "viewMode": "Geometry only", "highlightArc": 2 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Partition into n arcs",
      "narration": "Mark points P₁, P₂, …, Pₙ₋₁ along the curve. The kth arc, from Pₖ₋₁ to Pₖ, has arc length Δsₖ and carries mass ΔMₖ.",
      "state": { "numSegments": 8, "viewMode": "Geometry only", "highlightArc": 5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Zoom in on one arc",
      "narration": "Magnifying the kth arc shows it is nearly straight and short. Pick any point Pₖ*(xₖ*, yₖ*, zₖ*) on it — drag the red dot to see that the choice is free.",
      "state": { "numSegments": 8, "viewMode": "Show density f", "highlightArc": 5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Approximate the mass of one arc",
      "narration": "Over such a tiny arc the density is nearly constant, so ΔMₖ ≈ f(xₖ*, yₖ*, zₖ*) Δsₖ — density at the sample point times arc length.",
      "state": { "numSegments": 8, "viewMode": "Show density f", "highlightArc": 5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sum over all arcs",
      "narration": "Adding these approximations gives the Riemann sum Σ f(Pₖ*)Δsₖ, an estimate of the total mass of the wire.",
      "state": { "numSegments": 14, "viewMode": "Show Riemann sum", "highlightArc": 5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Let n → ∞",
      "narration": "Push n higher: the arcs shrink, the choice of sample point stops mattering, and the sum converges to the line integral ∫_C f ds.",
      "state": { "numSegments": 60, "viewMode": "Show Riemann sum", "highlightArc": 5 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A line integral is just a Riemann sum along a curve: mass ≈ (density at any sample point) × (tiny arc length), and in the limit the arbitrariness of the sample point washes out.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Layout mirrors the textbook: a large blue (#1a9fe0) smooth curve occupies the left two-thirds, running from a labeled endpoint 'P = P₀' at lower left, sweeping upward through a loop in the upper-left region, and exiting to the upper right at 'Q = Pₙ'. Solid blue dots mark partition points, labeled in serif italic math (P₁, P₂, P₃, Pₖ₋₁, Pₖ, Pₙ₋₁) placed just outside the curve with thin black leader arrows where crowded. The kth arc is emphasized (thicker stroke / darker blue) and carries a Δsₖ measurement bracket — two short vertical ticks joined by a double-headed arrow — plus a 'Mass ΔMₖ' caption with a leader arrow. A wide tan/cream ribbon arrow with a gray outline curves from the kth arc down to the lower-right inset. The inset shows the same arc greatly enlarged: a gentle blue arc with blue endpoint dots labeled Pₖ₋₁ and Pₖ, its own Δsₖ bracket above, and a red dot on the arc labeled Pₖ*(xₖ*, yₖ*, zₖ*) — this red dot is the draggable element, constrained to slide along the arc. A gray-bordered box at bottom center holds the formula ΔMₖ ≈ f(xₖ*, yₖ*, zₖ*) Δsₖ. In 'Show density f' mode, color the curve with a warm-to-cool gradient representing f and draw small vertical density bars at each sample point; in 'Show Riemann sum' mode add a small readout panel giving Σ f(Pₖ*)Δsₖ and the exact ∫_C f ds so convergence is visible as n increases. Keep typography serif-italic math, thin black annotation lines, white background."
}

